Enhanced ground grass: ao-vk
============================

The optional Enhanced ground-grass renderer (crates/ao-render/src/grass.rs,
grass.wgsl, grass_noise.rs) ports algorithms and shader code from ao-vk's
procedural grass (rvk/grass.cpp, rvk/shaders/grass.vert, grass_common.glsl,
wind.glsl): the camera-facing four-section blade strips and their kinds (blade,
broad blade, seed stalk, flower), tuft clumping and fan-out, dry/tall patches,
wildflower patches, slope and path-edge thinning, root-to-tip shading with canopy
darkening, light through the blade, rounded-edge shading and tangent/half-vector
physical sheen, wind waves and lighter gust bands, the character push, the minimum
drawn blade width and the smooth distance thinning. Placement and colour are
driven by this project's own terrain data instead. Tufts, species, native-mask
path thinning, gust light bands and sunlight shading are independently opt-in.
No ao-vk artwork or binary asset is used; the blade/species shapes and colours are
generated procedurally at draw time, only in the optional Enhanced grass path.

The optional grass sun-shadow caster adapts ao-vk's separate nearest-cascade
grass depth layer (rvk/shadow.cpp, grass.cpp and shaders/grass_shadow.vert).
Keeping grass depth separate lets receivers soften its contribution instead of
darkening the whole field: ao-vk's grass.vert uses 0.4, and its
ffp_main.glsl GrassShadowVisibility uses the 0.45 weight used by this renderer.
This renderer reuses the visible blade geometry, including its wind and
admitted-character deformation, rather than maintaining a second blade generator.
The independently optional grass point-light receiver in point_shadows.wgsl
reuses the renderer's cached static/actor cube sampling; blades do not cast into
point cubes. Receiver algorithms follow rvk/pointshadow.cpp and
shaders/grass.vert. The shared admitted-light pool, current source ownership
checks and native per-corner surviving lamp-energy cap are specific to this
renderer: shadows remove only local light surviving the native
saturation/Gouraud/ambient pipeline, before palette and fog. The MIT notice below
applies to these adaptations as well.
The per-frame GPU blade/section cache in grass.wgsl is specific to this renderer.
It evaluates the same procedural root, placement, species and native lighting
expressions once per visible blade prefix, then computes each blade section's
shared wind/push axis, both edge positions, sunlight and native fog once.
Beauty and every depth caster reuse those exact current-frame positions.

